<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Adab</h1><p class="st-meta">2700 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.) · capital Adab</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sumerian city on the Iturungal canal, devoted to the mother goddess Ninhursag and known mainly from its temple and administrative archives.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Adab, modern Bismaya, was occupied from the early third millennium BCE and became an independent city-state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. A late literary tradition credits a king of Adab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-anne-mundu">Lugal-Anne-mundu</a>, with vast conquests, but these stories cannot be confirmed by contemporary records.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its main temple, the E-sar, belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a> in her local form Digirmah. Excavations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-james-banks">Edgar Banks</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> in 1903 to 1905 found temple remains, statues and tablets, and many more Adab texts later entered collections through looting. They show a temple-centred economy of barley, livestock and craft workshops typical of Sumerian cities. Meskigal governed the city first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> and then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Adab lost its independence to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> around 2334 BCE and rebelled more than once against Akkadian kings. It remained a provincial city under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> and faded in the early second millennium BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 2700 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:5.97%;width:7.28%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2700 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/28th-century-bce">28th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adab</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meskigal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sumerian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Udnun, Bismaya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2517&amp;focus=adab" aria-label="Adab on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5863 1015 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Adab lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6183 1271L6129 1232"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6183 1271h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6183 1271h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Adab. <a href="/atlas?year=-2517&amp;focus=adab">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

